We then investigated the technical headers of messages.

An email header contains technical information about how the message travelled through mail systems.

Important headers we encountered included:
Authentication-Results
Received
X-Forefront-Antispam-Report
X-Microsoft-Antispam
X-MS-Exchange-Organization-SCL

Rather than trying to memorize every header, I learned to identify the ones relevant to an investigation.
SPF

SPF = Sender Policy Framework

Simple explanation:

SPF asks:

“Is this mail server authorized to send email for this domain?”
Our external Gmail test produced: SPF = PASS.
This meant the sending infrastructure passed Gmail’s SPF authorization check.

DKIM


DKIM = DomainKeys Identified Mail

DKIM provides a cryptographic signature associated with the sending domain.

In simple terms: DMARC = PASS



“Does this email have a valid digital signature from the domain?”
Our external Gmail test showed:DKIM = PASS
The signature was successfully verified.

DMARC:

DMARC = Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting & Conformance

DMARC uses authentication results and domain alignment to determine whether a message satisfies the sending domain’s authentication policy.
Our test showed:DMARC = PASS

